#import "@local/fgbooks:0.14.0": speech, stanza

== THE FOURTH STAGE

Now when they had eaten and drunk, and had chatted a little longer, their guide said to them, The day wears away; if you think it good, let us prepare to be going. So they got up to go, and the little boys went before; but Christiana forgot to take her bottle of spirits with her, so she sent her little boy back to fetch it. Then said Mercy, I think this is a losing place: here Christian lost his roll, and here Christiana left her bottle behind her. Sir, what is the cause of this? So their guide made answer, and said, The cause is sleep, or forgetfulness: some sleep when they should keep awake, and some forget when they should remember; and this is the very reason why often, at the resting-places, some pilgrims in some things come off losers. Pilgrims should watch, and remember what they have already received, in the midst of their greatest enjoyments; but for lack of doing so, often their rejoicing ends in tears, and their sunshine in a cloud: witness the story of Christian at this place.

When they had come to the place where Mistrust and Timorous met Christian, to persuade him to go back for fear of the lions, they saw what looked like a stage, and before it, towards the road, a broad plate with some verses written on it, and underneath, the reason given for raising up that stage in that place. The verses were,

#quote[“Let him that sees this stage, take heed \
Unto his heart and tongue; \
Lest, if he do not, here he speed \
As some have long agone.”]

The words underneath the verses were, “This stage was built to punish on it those who, through timorousness or mistrust, shall be afraid to go further on pilgrimage. Also, on this stage both Mistrust and Timorous were burned through the tongue with a hot iron, for trying to hinder Christian on his journey.”

Then said Mercy, This is much like the saying of the Beloved: “What will He do to you, and what will be added to you, O deceitful tongue? Sharp arrows will come from the warrior, with burning coals of the broom tree!” Ps 120:3, 4.

So they went on till they came within sight of the lions. Now Mr. Great-Heart was a strong man, so he was not afraid of a lion: but yet when they had come up to the place where the lions were, the boys, who went before, were now glad to cringe behind, for they were afraid of the lions; so they stepped back, and went behind. At this their guide smiled, and said, How now, my boys; do you love to go before when no danger approaches, and love to come behind as soon as the lions appear?

Now, as they went on, Mr. Great-heart drew his sword, intending to make a way for the pilgrims in spite of the lions. Then there appeared one who, it seems, had taken it upon himself to back the lions; and he said to the pilgrims’ guide, What is the reason for your coming here? Now the name of that man was Grim, or Bloody-man, because of his slaying of pilgrims; and he was of the race of the giants.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Then said the pilgrims’ guide, These women and children are going on pilgrimage, and this is the way they must go; and go it they shall, in spite of you and the lions.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Grim]: This is not their way, nor shall they go in it. I have come out to withstand them, and to that end will back the lions.]

Now, to tell the truth, because of the fierceness of the lions, and the grim bearing of him who backed them, this way had of late lain much unoccupied, and was almost grown over with grass.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Then said Christiana, Though the highways have been unoccupied before now, and though the travelers have been made in times past to walk through by-paths, it must not be so now that I have arisen, now that I have arisen a mother in Israel. Judg 5:6, 7.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Grim]: Then he swore by the lions that it should be so; and therefore told them to turn aside, for they should not have passage there.]

But Great-Heart their guide made his approach to Grim first, and laid on him so heavily with his sword that he forced him to retreat.

#speech[#smallcaps[Grim]: Then said he who attempted to back the lions, Will you slay me on my own ground?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: It is the King’s highway that we are in, and it is in this way that you have placed the lions; but these women, and these children, though weak, shall hold on their way in spite of your lions. And with that he gave him another downright blow, and brought him to his knees. With this blow also he broke his helmet, and with the next he cut off an arm. Then the giant roared so hideously that his voice frightened the women, and yet they were glad to see him lie sprawling on the ground. Now the lions were chained, and so of themselves could do nothing. So, when old Grim, who intended to back them, was dead, Mr. Great-Heart said to the pilgrims, Come now, and follow me, and no hurt shall happen to you from the lions. They therefore went on, but the women trembled as they passed by them; the boys also looked as if they would die; but they all got by without further hurt.]

Now, when they were within sight of the Porter’s lodge, they soon came up to it; but they made the more haste after this to go there, because it is dangerous traveling there in the night. So when they had come to the gate, the guide knocked, and the Porter cried, Who is there? But as soon as the guide had said, It is I, he knew his voice, and came down, for the guide had often before that come there as a conductor of pilgrims. When he had come down, he opened the gate; and seeing the guide standing just before it (for he did not see the women, for they were behind him), he said to him, How now, Mr. Great-Heart, what is your business here so late at night? I have brought, said he, some pilgrims here, where, by my Lord’s commandment, they must lodge: I would have been here some time ago, had I not been opposed by the giant who used to back the lions. But after a long and tedious combat with him, I have cut him off, and have brought the pilgrims here in safety.

#speech[#smallcaps[The Porter]: Will you not come in, and stay till morning?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: No, I will return to my Lord tonight.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Oh, sir, I do not know how to be willing that you should leave us in our pilgrimage: you have been so faithful and loving to us, you have fought so stoutly for us, you have been so hearty in counseling us, that I shall never forget your favor towards us.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Then said Mercy, Oh, that we might have your company to our journey’s end! How can such poor women as we hold out in a way so full of troubles as this way is, without a friend and defender?]

#speech[#smallcaps[James]: Then said James, the youngest of the boys, Please, sir, be persuaded to go with us, and help us, because we are so weak, and the way is so dangerous as it is.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: I am at my Lord’s commandment; if he shall assign me to be your guide all the way through, I will willingly wait on you. But here you failed at first; for when he told me to come this far with you, then you should have begged him to let me go all the way through with you, and he would have granted your request. However, at present I must withdraw; and so, good Christiana, Mercy, and my brave children, adieu.]

Then the Porter, Mr. Watchful, asked Christiana about her country, and about her kindred. And she said, I came from the city of Destruction. I am a widow woman, and my husband is dead; his name was Christian, the pilgrim. What! said the Porter, was he your husband? Yes, said she, and these are his children; and this, pointing to Mercy, is one of my townswomen. Then the Porter rang his bell, as he is used to doing at such times, and there came to the door one of the damsels, whose name was Humble-Mind; and to her the Porter said, Go tell it within, that Christiana, the wife of Christian, and her children, have come here on pilgrimage. She went in, therefore, and told it. But oh, what a noise for gladness there was within when the damsel had only let that drop out of her mouth!

So they came with haste to the Porter, for Christiana stood still at the door. Then some of the most grave said to her, Come in, Christiana, come in, you wife of that good man; come in, you blessed woman, come in, with all who are with you. So she went in, and those who were her children and companions followed her. Now when they had gone in, they were taken into a large room, where they were told to sit down: so they sat down, and the chief of the house were called to see and welcome the guests. Then they came in, and understanding who they were, greeted each other with a kiss, and said, Welcome, you#super[+] vessels of the grace of God; welcome to us, your friends.

Now, because it was somewhat late, and because the pilgrims were weary with their journey, and also made faint with the sight of the fight, and of the terrible lions, they desired, as soon as might be, to prepare to go to rest. No, said those of the family, refresh yourselves first with a morsel of food; for they had prepared for them a lamb, with the accustomed sauce belonging to it, Exod 12:21; John 1:29; for the Porter had heard before of their coming, and had told it to them within. So when they had eaten supper, and ended their prayer with a psalm, they desired that they might go to rest.

But let us, said Christiana, if we may be so bold as to choose, be in that chamber that was my husband’s when he was here; so they took them up there, and they all lay in one room. When they were at rest, Christiana and Mercy entered into conversation about things that were fitting.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Little did I think once, when my husband went on pilgrimage, that I should ever have followed him.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: And you as little thought of lying in his bed, and in his chamber to rest, as you do now.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: And much less did I ever think of seeing his face with comfort, and of worshiping the Lord the King with him; and yet now I believe I shall.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Hark, don’t you hear a noise?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Yes, it is, as I believe, a noise of music, for joy that we are here.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Wonderful! Music in the house, music in the heart, and music also in heaven, for joy that we are here! Thus they talked a while, and then went to sleep.]

So in the morning when they were awake, Christiana said to Mercy, What was the matter, that you laughed in your sleep last night? I suppose you were in a dream.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: So I was, and a sweet dream it was; but are you sure I laughed?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Yes, you laughed heartily; but please, Mercy, tell me your dream.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: I was dreaming that I sat all alone in a solitary place, and was bemoaning the hardness of my heart. Now I had not sat there long before it seemed to me that many were gathered about me to see me, and to hear what it was that I said. So they listened, and I went on bemoaning the hardness of my heart. At this, some of them laughed at me, some called me fool, and some began to push me about. With that, I thought I looked up and saw one coming towards me with wings. So he came directly to me, and said, Mercy, what ails you? Now when he had heard me make my complaint, he said, Peace be to you; he also wiped my eyes with his handkerchief, and clothed me in silver and gold. Ezek 16:8–11. He put a chain about my neck, and earrings in my ears, and a beautiful crown upon my head. Then he took me by the hand, and said, Mercy, come after me. So he went up, and I followed till we came to a golden gate. Then he knocked; and when those within had opened, the man went in, and I followed him up to a throne, upon which one sat; and he said to me, Welcome, daughter. The place looked bright and twinkling, like the stars, or rather like the sun, and I thought that I saw your husband there; so I awoke from my dream. But did I laugh?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Laugh! Yes, and well you might, to see yourself so well. For you must give me leave to tell you that it was a good dream; and that, as you have begun to find the first part true, so you shall find the second at last. “God speaks in one way and in another, yet no one notices: in a dream, in a vision in the night, when deep sleep falls upon men as they slumber on their beds.” Job 33:14, 15. We need not, when in bed, lie awake to talk with God; he can visit us while we sleep, and cause us then to hear his voice. Our heart often wakes when we sleep, and God can speak to that, either by words, by proverbs, by signs and similitudes, as well as if one were awake.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Well, I am glad of my dream; for I hope before long to see it fulfilled, to the making me laugh again.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: I think it is now high time to rise, and to know what we must do.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Please, if they invite us to stay a while, let us willingly accept the offer. I am the more willing to stay a while here, to grow better acquainted with these maids: I think Prudence, Piety, and Charity have very comely and sober countenances.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: We shall see what they will do.]

So when they were up and ready, they came down, and they asked one another about their rest, and if it was comfortable or not.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Very good, said Mercy: it was one of the best night’s lodgings that I ever had in my life.]

Then said Prudence and Piety, If you will be persuaded to stay here a while, you shall have what the house can afford.

#speech[#smallcaps[Charity]: Yes, and that with a very good will, said Charity. So they consented, and stayed there about a month or more, and were very helpful to one another. And because Prudence wanted to see how Christiana had brought up her children, she asked her leave to catechize them. So she gave her free consent. Then she began with her youngest, whose name was James.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: And she said, Come, James, can you tell me who made you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[James]: God the Father, God the Son, and God the Holy Spirit.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: Good boy. And can you tell who saved you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[James]: God the Father, God the Son, and God the Holy Spirit.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: Good boy still. But how does God the Father save you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[James]: By his grace.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: How does God the Son save you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[James]: By his righteousness, death and blood, and life.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: And how does God the Holy Spirit save you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[James]: By his illumination, by his renovation, and by his preservation.]

Then said Prudence to Christiana, You are to be commended for bringing up your children like this. I suppose I need not ask the rest these questions, since the youngest of them can answer them so well. I will therefore now apply myself to the next youngest.

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: Then she said, Come, Joseph (for his name was Joseph), will you let me catechize you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Joseph]: With all my heart.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: What is man?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Joseph]: A reasonable creature, so made by God, as my brother said.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: What is implied by this word, saved?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Joseph]: That man, by sin, has brought himself into a state of captivity and misery.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: What is implied by his being saved by the Trinity?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Joseph]: That sin is so great and mighty a tyrant that none can pull us out of its clutches but God; and that God is so good and loving to man as to pull him indeed out of this miserable state.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: What is God’s design in saving poor men?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Joseph]: The glorifying of his name, of his grace, and justice, etc., and the everlasting happiness of his creature.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: Who are they that will be saved?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Joseph]: Those who accept his salvation.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: Good boy, Joseph; your mother has taught you well, and you have listened to what she has said to you.]

Then said Prudence to Samuel, who was the eldest but one,

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: Come, Samuel, are you willing that I should catechize you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Samuel]: Yes, indeed, if you please.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: What is heaven?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Samuel]: A place and state most blessed, because God dwells there.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: What is hell?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Samuel]: A place and state most woeful, because it is the dwelling-place of sin, the devil, and death.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: Why would you go to heaven?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Samuel]: That I may see God, and serve him without weariness; that I may see Christ, and love him everlastingly; that I may have that fullness of the Holy Spirit in me which I can by no means enjoy here.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: A very good boy, and one who has learned well.]

Then she addressed herself to the eldest, whose name was Matthew; and she said to him, Come, Matthew, shall I also catechize you?

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: With a very good will.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: I ask then, if there was ever anything that had a being antecedent to, or before, God?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: No, for God is eternal; nor is there anything, except himself, that had a being until the beginning of the first day. For in six days the Lord made heaven and earth, the sea, and all that is in them.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: What do you think of the Bible?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: It is the holy word of God.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: Is there nothing written in it but what you understand?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: Yes, a great deal.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: What do you do when you meet with places in it that you do not understand?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: I think God is wiser than I. I pray also that he will be pleased to let me know all in it that he knows will be for my good.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: What do you believe concerning the resurrection of the dead?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: I believe they shall rise the same as were buried; the same in nature, though not in corruption. And I believe this on a double account: first, because God has promised it; secondly, because he is able to perform it.]

Then said Prudence to the boys, You must still listen to your mother; for she can teach you more. You must also diligently give ear to what good talk you shall hear from others: for your sakes they speak good things. Observe also, and that with carefulness, what the heavens and the earth teach you; but especially be much in the meditation of that book which was the cause of your father’s becoming a pilgrim. I, for my part, my children, will teach you what I can while you are here, and shall be glad if you will ask me questions that tend to godly edifying.

Now when these pilgrims had been at this place a week, Mercy had a visitor who professed some good will towards her, and his name was Mr. Brisk; a man of some breeding, and who professed religion, but a man who stuck very close to the world. So he came once or twice, or more, to Mercy, and offered her his love. Now Mercy was of a fair countenance, and therefore the more alluring.

Her mind also was to be always busying herself in doing; for when she had nothing to do for herself, she would be making stockings and garments for others, and would bestow them on those who had need. And Mr. Brisk, not knowing where or how she disposed of what she made, seemed to be greatly taken, because he never found her idle. I will warrant she is a good housewife, said he to himself.

Mercy then revealed the business to the maidens who were of the house, and inquired of them about him, for they knew him better than she did. So they told her that he was a very busy young man, and one who professed religion, but was, as they feared, a stranger to the power of that which is good.

Well then, said Mercy, I will look no more on him; for I intend never to have a clog on my soul.

Prudence then replied that there was no need to give him any great discouragement; her continuing as she had begun to do for the poor would quickly cool his courage.

So the next time he comes he finds her at her old work, making things for the poor. Then said he, What, always at it? Yes, said she, either for myself or for others. And what can you earn a day? said he. I do these things, said she, that I may be rich in good works, laying up in store for myself a good foundation against the time to come, that I may lay hold on eternal life. 1 Tim 6:17–19. Why, tell me, what do you do with them? said he. Clothe the naked, said she. With that his countenance fell. So he stopped coming to her. And when he was asked the reason why, he said that Mercy was a pretty lass, but troubled with ill ways.

When he had left her, Prudence said, Did I not tell you that Mr. Brisk would soon forsake you? yes, he will raise up an ill report of you; for, notwithstanding his profession of religion, and his seeming love to Mercy, yet Mercy and he are of tempers so different that I believe they will never come together.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: I might have had husbands before now, though I did not speak of it to anyone; but they were such as did not like my ways, though none of them ever found fault with my person. So they and I could not agree.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: Mercy in our days is valued but little beyond its name: the practice which your ways set forth, there are but few who can abide.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Well, said Mercy, if nobody will have me, I will die unmarried, or my ways shall be to me as a husband: for I cannot change my nature; and to have one who goes against me in this, I intend never to allow as long as I live. I had a sister named Bountiful, who was married to one of these churls, but he and she could never agree; but because my sister was resolved to do as she had begun, that is, to show kindness to the poor, therefore her husband first cried her down at the market cross, and then turned her out of his doors.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: And yet he was a professing Christian, I warrant you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Yes, such a one as he was, and of such as he the world is now full: but I am for none of them all.]

Now Matthew, the eldest son of Christiana, fell sick, and his sickness was severe, for he was in great pain in his bowels, so that at times he was pulled with it, as it were, both ends together. There also lived not far from there one Mr. Skill, an aged and well-proven physician. So Christiana desired it, and they sent for him, and he came. When he had entered the room, and had observed the boy a little, he concluded that he was sick with the gripes. Then he said to his mother, What diet has Matthew fed on lately? Diet! said Christiana, nothing but what is wholesome. The physician answered, This boy has been tampering with something that lies in his stomach undigested, and that will not go away without means. And I tell you he must be purged, or else he will die.

#speech[#smallcaps[Samuel]: Then said Samuel, Mother, what was that which my brother gathered up and ate as soon as we had come from the gate that is at the head of this way? You know that there was an orchard on the left hand, on the other side of the wall, and some of the trees hung over the wall, and my brother plucked and ate.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: True, my child, said Christiana, he did take some, and ate it: naughty boy as he was, I scolded him, and yet he would eat it.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Skill]: I knew he had eaten something that was not wholesome food; and that food, namely that fruit, is the most hurtful of all. It is the fruit of Beelzebub’s orchard. I wonder that no one warned you of it; many have died of it.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Then Christiana began to cry; and she said, Oh, naughty boy! and Oh, careless mother! what shall I do for my son?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Skill]: Come, do not be too much dejected; the boy may do well again, but he must purge and vomit.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Please, sir, try the utmost of your skill with him, whatever it costs.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Skill]: Well, I hope I will be reasonable. So he made him a purge, but it was too weak; it was said it was made of the blood of a goat, the ashes of a heifer, and some of the juice of hyssop. Heb 9:13, 19; 10:1–4. When Mr. Skill had seen that that purge was too weak, he made one fit for the purpose. It was made #emph[ex carne et sanguine Christi],#footnote[Of the flesh and blood of Christ.] John 6:54–57; Heb 9:14; (you know physicians give strange medicines to their patients:) and it was made into pills, with a promise or two, and a proportionate quantity of salt. Mark 9:49. Now, he was to take them three at a time, fasting, in half a quarter of a pint of the tears of repentance. Zech 12:10.]

When this potion was prepared, and brought to the boy, he was loath to take it, though torn with the gripes as if he would be pulled in pieces. Come, come, said the physician, you must take it. It goes against my stomach, said the boy. I must have you take it, said his mother. I will vomit it up again, said the boy. Please, sir, said Christiana to Mr. Skill, how does it taste? It has no bad taste, said the doctor; and with that she touched one of the pills with the tip of her tongue. Oh, Matthew, said she, this potion is sweeter than honey. If you love your mother, if you love your brothers, if you love Mercy, if you love your life, take it. So, with much ado, after a short prayer for the blessing of God upon it, he took it, and it worked kindly with him. It caused him to purge; it caused him to sleep, and to rest quietly; it put him into a fine heat and breathing sweat, and quite rid him of his gripes. So in a little time he got up, and walked about with a staff, and would go from room to room, and talk with Prudence, Piety, and Charity of his illness, and how he was healed.

So when the boy was healed, Christiana asked Mr. Skill, saying, Sir, what will satisfy you for your pains and care to and for my child? And he said, You must pay the master of the College of Physicians, Heb 13:11–15, according to rules made in that case and provided.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: But, sir, said she, what else is this pill good for?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Skill]: It is a universal pill; it is good against all the diseases that pilgrims are subject to; and when it is well prepared, it will keep good, time out of mind.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Please, sir, make me up twelve boxes of them; for if I can get these, I will never take any other medicine.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Skill]: These pills are good to prevent diseases, as well as to cure when one is sick. Yes, I dare say it, and stand by it, that if a man will only use this medicine as he should, it will make him live forever. John 6:51. But, good Christiana, you must give these pills no other way but as I have prescribed; for if you do, they will do no good. So he gave Christiana medicine for herself, and her boys, and for Mercy; and told Matthew to take care how he ate any more green plums; and kissed them, and went his way.]

It was told you before that Prudence told the boys that if at any time they would, they should ask her some questions that might be profitable, and she would say something to them.

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: Then Matthew, who had been sick, asked her why, for the most part, medicine should be bitter to our palates.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: To show how unwelcome the word of God and its effects are to a carnal heart.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: Why does medicine, if it does good, purge, and cause one to vomit?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: To show that the word, when it works effectually, cleanses the heart and mind. For look, what the one does to the body, the other does to the soul.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: What should we learn by seeing the flame of our fire go upwards, and by seeing the beams and sweet influences of the sun strike downwards?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: By the going up of the fire, we are taught to ascend to heaven by fervent and hot desires. And by the sun sending his heat, beams, and sweet influences downwards, we are taught that the Savior of the world, though high, reaches down with his grace and love to us below.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: Where do the clouds get their water?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: Out of the sea.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: What may we learn from that?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: That ministers should fetch their doctrine from God.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: Why do they empty themselves upon the earth?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: To show that ministers should give out what they know of God to the world.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: Why is the rainbow caused by the sun?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: To show that the covenant of God’s grace is confirmed to us in Christ.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: Why do the springs come from the sea to us through the earth?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: To show that the grace of God comes to us through the body of Christ.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: Why do some of the springs rise out of the tops of high hills?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: To show that the Spirit of grace will spring up in some who are great and mighty, as well as in many who are poor and low.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: Why does the fire fasten upon the candle-wick?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: To show that unless grace kindles upon the heart, there will be no true light of life in us.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: Why are the wick, and tallow and all, spent to maintain the light of the candle?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: To show that body and soul, and all, should be at the service of, and spend themselves to maintain in good condition, that grace of God that is in us.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: Why does the pelican pierce her own breast with her bill?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: To nourish her young ones with her blood, and so to show that Christ the blessed so loved his young (his people) as to save them from death by his blood.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Matthew]: What may one learn by hearing the cock crow?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Prudence]: Learn to remember Peter’s sin, and Peter’s repentance. The cock’s crowing also shows that day is coming on: let, then, the crowing of the cock put you in mind of that last and terrible day of judgment.]

Now about this time their month was up; so they made known to those of the house that it was fitting for them to up and be going. Then Joseph said to his mother, It is proper that you do not forget to send to the house of Mr. Interpreter, to ask him to grant that Mr. Great-Heart should be sent to us, so that he may be our conductor for the rest of the way. Good boy, said she, I had almost forgotten. So she drew up a petition, and asked Mr. Watchful the porter to send it by some fit man to her good friend Mr. Interpreter; who, when it had come, and he had seen the contents of the petition, said to the messenger, Go, tell them that I will send him.

When the family where Christiana was saw that they had a purpose to go forward, they called the whole house together, to give thanks to their King for sending them such edifying guests as these. When that was done, they said to Christiana, And shall we not show you something, as our custom is to do to pilgrims, on which you may meditate when you are on the way? So they took Christiana, her children, and Mercy into the private room, and showed them one of the apples that Eve ate of, and that she also gave to her husband, and for the eating of which they were both turned out of paradise, and asked her what she thought that was. Then Christiana said, It is food or poison, I do not know which. So they explained the matter to her, and she held up her hands and wondered. Gen 3:6; Rom 7:24.

Then they took her to a place, and showed her Jacob’s ladder. Gen 28:12. Now at that time there were some angels ascending upon it. So Christiana looked and looked to see the angels go up: so did the rest of the company. Then they were going into another place, to show them something else; but James said to his mother, Please, ask them to stay here a little longer, for this is a rare sight. So they turned again, and stood feeding their eyes on so pleasant a view.

After this, they took them into a place where a golden anchor hung. So they told Christiana to take it down; for, said they, You shall have it with you, for it is absolutely necessary that you should, so that you may lay hold of that within the veil, Heb 6:19, and stand steadfast in case you should meet with turbulent weather, Joel 3:16: so they were glad of it.

Then they took them, and brought them to the mount upon which Abraham our father offered up Isaac his son, and showed them the altar, the wood, the fire, and the knife, for they remain to be seen to this very day. Gen 22:9. When they had seen it, they held up their hands, and blessed themselves, and said, Oh, what a man for love to his Master, and for denial of himself, was Abraham!

After they had shown them all these things, Prudence took them into a dining room, where stood a pair of excellent virginals;#footnote[A musical instrument.] so she played upon them, and turned what she had shown them into this excellent song, saying,

#quote[“Eve’s apple we have showed you; \
Of that be you aware: \
You have seen Jacob’s ladder too, \
Upon which angels are. \
An anchor you received have; \
But let not these suffice, \
Until with Abra’m you have gave \
Your best, a sacrifice.”]

Now, about this time, someone knocked at the door; so the Porter opened, and behold, Mr. Great-Heart was there. But when he had come in, what joy there was! for it now came afresh into their minds how only a little while ago he had slain old Grim Bloody-man the giant, and had delivered them from the lions.

Then Mr. Great-Heart said to Christiana and to Mercy, My Lord has sent each of you a bottle of wine, and also some parched corn, together with a couple of pomegranates; he has also sent the boys some figs and raisins, to refresh you on your way.

Then they set themselves to their journey, and Prudence and Piety went along with them. When they came to the gate, Christiana asked the Porter if anyone had gone by lately. He said, No; only one, some time ago, who also told me that lately there had been a great robbery committed on the King’s highway as you go. But, said he, the thieves are taken, and will shortly be tried for their lives. Then Christiana and Mercy were afraid; but Matthew said, Mother, fear nothing, as long as Mr. Great-Heart is to go with us, and to be our conductor.

Then Christiana said to the Porter, Sir, I am much obliged to you for all the kindnesses that you have shown to me since I came here; and also because you have been so loving and kind to my children. I do not know how to repay your kindness; so, please, as a token of my respect to you, accept this small mite. So she put a gold angel#footnote[A gold coin worth ten shillings.] in his hand; and he made her a low bow, and said, “Let your garments always be white, and let your head never lack oil.” Eccl 9:8. Let Mercy live and not die, and let not her works be few. Deut 33:6. And to the boys he said, Flee youthful lusts, and follow after godliness with those who are grave and wise, 2 Tim 2:22: so you will put gladness into your mother’s heart, and obtain praise from all who are sober-minded. So they thanked the Porter, and departed.
